},{"id":"CovetCovetousness","title":"Covet, Covetousness*"},{"id":"MicahBookof","title":"Micah, Book of"},{"id":"Power","title":"Power"},{"id":"Prophecy","title":"Prophecy"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jacob","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jacob::Jacob::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jacob"},"text":"Should it be said, O house of Jacob, “Is the Spirit of the LORD impatient? Are these the things He does?” Do not My words bring good to him who walks uprightly?","verse":7},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jacob","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jacob::Jacob::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jacob"},"text":"I will surely gather all of you, O Jacob; I will collect the remnant of Israel. I will bring them together like sheep in a pen, like a flock in the midst of its pasture—a noisy throng.","verse":12}],"id":"Jacob","label":"Jacob","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Jacob::Jacob::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Jacob","term":"Jacob"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Jacob","sourceTitle":"Jacob"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Therefore this is what the LORD says: “I am planning against this nation a disaster from which you cannot free your necks. Then you will not walk so proudly, for it will be a time of calamity.","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Therefore, you will have no one in the assembly of the LORD to divide the land by lot.","verse":5},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Should it be said, O house of Jacob, “Is the Spirit of the LORD impatient? Are these the things He does?” Do not My words bring good to him who walks uprightly?","verse":7},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"One who breaks open the way will go up before them; they will break through the gate, and go out by it. Their King will pass through before them, the LORD as their leader.","verse":13}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.1.2-2.13","r":"Mic.1.2-2.13","rawR":"Mic.1.2-2.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.1.2-2.13","value":"Mic.1.2-2.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:2–2:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This message of judgment introduces some of the major concerns of Micah’s prophecies and asserts God’s determination to judge his people and put them into exile (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.1.16","r":"Mic.1.16","rawR":"Mic.1.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.1.16","value":"Mic.1.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.4-5","r":"Mic.2.4-5","rawR":"Mic.2.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.4-5","value":"Mic.2.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:4-5"},"), but it concludes with the Lord’s assurance that he will rescue a remnant from exile (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.12-13","r":"Mic.2.12-13","rawR":"Mic.2.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.12-13","value":"Mic.2.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:12-13"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.1.2-2.13","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.1.2-2.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d388f6058b8ef47b39a8bb3865c44a8e640c1ba21d52cf0b22c9a5f4c20b4f2c","sourceItemName":"Mic.1.2-2.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11246,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.1.2-2.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.1.2-2.13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.1-2","r":"Mic.2.1-2","rawR":"Mic.2.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.1-2","value":"Mic.2.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:1-2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Power"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," had corrupted the wealthy, who should have been ready to help their fellow Israelites (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.4.9","r":"Gen.4.9","rawR":"Gen.4.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.4.9","value":"Gen.4.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 4:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.1.14","r":"Josh.1.14","rawR":"Josh.1.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.1.14","value":"Josh.1.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 1:14"},"). • ",{"c":["Thinking up evil plans . . . because you have the power to do so"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," indicates a corrupt heart, mind, and character (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.6.5","r":"Gen.6.5","rawR":"Gen.6.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.6.5","value":"Gen.6.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 6:5"},"). • ",{"c":["When you want: "],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"They possessed the ",{"c":["property"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of others in a way that amounted to stealing and broke God’s law that forbids coveting (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.20.17","r":"Exod.20.17","rawR":"Exod.20.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.20.17","value":"Exod.20.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 20:17"},"). A ",{"c":["family’s inheritance"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was a sacred gift from the Lord, intended as a permanent possession (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.25.8-55","r":"Lev.25.8-55","rawR":"Lev.25.8-55","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.25.8-55","value":"Lev.25.8-55"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 25:8-55"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.21.1-29","r":"1Kgs.21.1-29","rawR":"1Kgs.21.1-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.21.1-29","value":"1Kgs.21.1-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.5.8","r":"Isa.5.8","rawR":"Isa.5.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.5.8","value":"Isa.5.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 5:8"},"). God looked for righteousness among his people, but instead he found oppression (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.10.9","r":"1Kgs.10.9","rawR":"1Kgs.10.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.10.9","value":"1Kgs.10.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 10:9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.5.7","r":"Isa.5.7","rawR":"Isa.5.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.5.7","value":"Isa.5.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 5:7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Pet.2.13","r":"2Pet.2.13","rawR":"2Pet.2.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Pet.2.13","value":"2Pet.2.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Pet 2:13"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.1-2","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.1-2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"6fb406979b3401b42969cc4be2a07d940efa538512f1c5f3bd31c25ba44cdd71","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.1-2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11262,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.1-2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.1-2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.3-5","r":"Mic.2.3-5","rawR":"Mic.2.3-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.3-5","value":"Mic.2.3-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:3-5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The Lord, the Judge, reads out the sentence. He would pay back his people’s ",{"c":["evil"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," hearts and actions ",{"c":["with evil"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," in kind. The prophet is engaging in wordplay here. The Hebrew word translated “evil” has a wide range of meaning. It can connote moral evil, as in the first instance; it can also connote calamity or disaster as in the second instance. The Lord would bring calamity on them in response to their wickedness."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.3-5","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.3-5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"1737fcf5dd81a858693d7e99796226ded987d66786955a6cda719ef12fe6863d","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.3-5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11263,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.3-5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.3-5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.4","r":"Mic.2.4","rawR":"Mic.2.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.4","value":"Mic.2.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The power brokers would be ",{"c":["ruined"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," financially as their enemies confiscated their property. The land that they had seized unjustly from fellow Israelites would be violently taken from them (",{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.5","r":"Mic.2.5","rawR":"Mic.2.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.5","value":"Mic.2.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:5"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.4","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"84525bd04fb2ea88ee78f8bf40023da07255d88bea5ea23d02d7f66d8e6f963e","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11264,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.6","r":"Mic.2.6","rawR":"Mic.2.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.6","value":"Mic.2.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," There was a pathetic attempt to stifle the words of a true prophet. • ",{"c":["the people respond:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," They thought that exile and other ",{"c":["such disasters"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," could not happen to them, but they were wrong."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.6","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8ae50f74028907f878918d7f24e354dcac97687c435d8d156ea2f4cc1c6a6316","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11265,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.7-10","r":"Mic.2.7-10","rawR":"Mic.2.7-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.7-10","value":"Mic.2.7-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:7-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The maltreatment of their fellow Israelites was tantamount to an attack against the Lord and his prophet. The fault lay with the people, not with Micah’s inspired and righteous message."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.7-10","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.7-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"aecfae3d84f5ed96d391819f9f4511091d284ce9ee165e54691ad19f1d4603c8","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.7-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11266,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.7-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.7-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.11","r":"Mic.2.11","rawR":"Mic.2.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.11","value":"Mic.2.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"},{"c":[" a prophet full of lies:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," These evil people loved to hear deceptions from their favorite good-time prophets (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.28.8-9","r":"Jer.28.8-9","rawR":"Jer.28.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.28.8-9","value":"Jer.28.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 28:8-9"},"). False prophets proclaimed assurance that Israel and Judah could escape from judgment. When judgment came, they had no comfort to give."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.11","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ba295ff2492a7415bf72d9aeee033e686835df0946beaf0eb4828013ce8ecd32","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11267,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.12-13","r":"Mic.2.12-13","rawR":"Mic.2.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.12-13","value":"Mic.2.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:12-13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," God showed his love and care for his rebellious people by giving them a promise of hope even as he spoke of exile and despair. Israel would be scattered, but they would be brought back (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.4.26-29","r":"Deut.4.26-29","rawR":"Deut.4.26-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.4.26-29","value":"Deut.4.26-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 4:26-29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.30.1-6","r":"Deut.30.1-6","rawR":"Deut.30.1-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.30.1-6","value":"Deut.30.1-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"30:1-6"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.12-13","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.12-13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"672d1119b343e37091a97d7c396e290d6444d48e502418d35c78b5bf139286f2","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.12-13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11268,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.12-13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.12-13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Mic.2.13","r":"Mic.2.13","rawR":"Mic.2.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mic.2.13","value":"Mic.2.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["The"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"}," did lead Israel ",{"c":["out of exile"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," foreshadowing the even greater freedom from slavery that the Messiah, Jesus Christ, would later bring."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Mic.2.13","refs":[{"book":"MIC","endChapter":2,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Mic.2.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"MIC","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"01e8018c055984a280d8b2fe1de93922ff21a173c0f7496e971435a483f80f0c","sourceItemName":"Mic.2.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11269,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"MIC","canonicalOSIS":"Mic","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Mic"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Mic.2.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Mic.2.13","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}